<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Malawi</h1><p class="st-meta">1964 CE – present · capital Lilongwe</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A landlocked country in south-east Africa, independent from Britain in 1964, under Hastings Banda's one-party rule until 1993 and a multi-party republic since.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nyasaland">Nyasaland</a> became independent from British rule on 6 July 1964 and was renamed Malawi, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hastings-banda">Hastings Banda</a>, leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malawi-congress-party">Malawi Congress Party</a> (MCP), as prime minister. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federation-of-rhodesia-and-nyasaland">Federation of Rhodesia and Nyasaland</a>, which Banda had opposed, was dissolved in 1963.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Under a new constitution Malawi became a republic in 1966 with Banda as its first president, and the MCP was made the only legal party. In 1971 he was declared president for life, and his regime was rigidly totalitarian, though Malawi suffered no armed conflict. Under pressure, Banda agreed to a referendum in 1993, in which the people voted for a multi-party system, and he lost the 1994 election to Bakili Muluzi.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Bingu wa Mutharika was elected in 2004 and re-elected in 2009, and in July 2011 protests left 18 people dead. After his death in April 2012, Joyce Banda became president. Peter Mutharika won in 2014, but the Constitutional Court overturned his 2019 re-election because of irregularities and the Supreme Court upheld the decision. Lazarus Chakwera won the new election in 2020, and Mutharika returned to power in the 2025 general election.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/malawi.jpg" alt="Malawi" width="330" height="220"><figcaption>Image: Original: Unknown author Vector: Achim1999 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Malawi.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1964 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lilongwe</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hastings Banda, Bakili Muluzi, Bingu wa Mutharika, Joyce Banda, Peter Mutharika</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>mostly Christian (Roman Catholic Church, Church of Central Africa Presbyterian), a Muslim minority</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, Chichewa, Chitumbuka, Chiyao</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/malawi">Malawi</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Malawi</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1995&amp;focus=malawi" aria-label="Malawi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: where Malawi lay"><image href="/maps/globes/g03000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5312 6184L5438 6300"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5438 6300h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5312 6184h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5312 6184h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Lilongwe. <a href="/atlas?year=1995&amp;focus=malawi">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
